    "case_id": "397",
    "case_summary": "A man in his 80s presented to the emergency department after 1 hour of chest pain and vomiting. The patient denied any history of hypertension and diabetes.\n\nTemperature: 36°CHeart rate: 79 beats per minute Blood pressure: 92/56 mm HgThe maximal cardiac impulse was noted to be in the right fifth interspaceHepatic dullness located in the left subcostal region\n\n- Laboratory Tests:Initial serum troponin I level: 0.070 ng/mL (normal range, ≤0.028 ng/mL)- ECG Findings:Initial ECG showed:- Negative P waves in lead I- QRS complexes in leads V1 through V6 showing activation of ventricle in right thorax- Diminishing QRS amplitude progression from V1 to V6- ST-segment elevations in leads I, II, III, aVF, and V4 through V6- Imaging Studies:1. Coronary Angiography:Finding: Total occlusion in the proximal right coronary artery2. Transthoracic Echocardiogram:Finding: Right atrium and right ventricle lay on the left side, left atrium and left ventricle lay on the right side",
